// The terraingen package (a working name; Jon names packages): landscapes from parameters. A program
// reaches it with 'import terraingen' and describes a landscape as DATA, a TerrainSpec, then asks for it:
//
//   imm spec = alpine(512usize, 512usize, 10., 7u32);      // 512 x 512 samples, 10 m apart, seed 7
//   imm land = buildLandscape(&spec);                      // on the CPU; buildLandscapeOn(&mut device, &spec) erodes on a GPU
//   land.heightAt(x, z);   land.normalAt(x, z);   land.waterDepthAt(x, z);
//   cutTile(&land.height, tx, tz, 33usize, 1usize);        // tiles with shared edges, for a terrain renderer
//   groundSignals(&land.height, &land.masks, x0, z0, 256., 128u32);   // the land's share of groundcover's eight signals
//
// "Here is a picture of a mountain range; do something like it" becomes choosing the fields of a
// TerrainSpec: 'alpine', 'mesa' and 'hills' are three such choices (landscape.cone), each about a
// kilobyte of numbers.
//
// WHAT IT DOES, in the order the pipeline runs (Landscape, landscape.cone):
//
//   1. GENERATE (gpu/shape.cone, generate.cone). A ShapeParams is a recipe of at most four fractal
//      layers (fBm with Quilez's derivative damping, Musgrave's ridged multifractal, billow), each
//      optionally gated by another layer; a domain warp (Quilez); then shaping: a power curve, terraces,
//      a plateau cap, a radial island falloff, a final detail octave. A pure function of (parameters, x,
//      z) over 32-bit numbers, so it is also the kernel 'generate' and runs on the GPU through gpuwork,
//      within 3 mm of the CPU's (the GPU's sin and exp differ by a few ulps).
//
//   2. ERODE (gpu/erosion.cone, erode.cone). HYDRAULIC erosion by the virtual pipe model (Mei, Decaudin
//      and Hu 2007): water, suspended sediment and flux through four pipes a cell, five kernels an
//      iteration; THERMAL erosion by talus (Musgrave, Kolb and Mace 1989) in the gathering form of Jako
//      and Toth (2011). Each kernel reads what it does not write, so the order of invocations cannot
//      matter, and 'erodeCpu' (the kernels' own functions in a loop) and 'erodeGpu' (one gpuwork Job for
//      the whole plan, the data never returning in between) agree: the mean difference measured is
//      1e-6 m.
//
//   3. DRAINAGE, RIVERS, LAKES (hydrology.cone). Priority-Flood with epsilon (Barnes et al. 2014) fills
//      the depressions; D8 flow directions and flow accumulation give the drainage; depressions become
//      lakes (a shallow one is a puddle the water runs through, a deep one a dry basin that drains to its
//      own pit); channels where the drained area passes a threshold are carved to a depth and width that
//      grow with its square root.
//
//   4. MASKS AND BIOMES (masks.cone). Rasters on the heightfield's grid: slope, moisture (humidity, the
//      logarithm of the drained area, nearness to water), sun exposure (a horizon march), flow, river,
//      lake, distance to water (a chamfer transform), curvature, and biome weights from a data table of
//      windows on slope, height and moisture. 'bakeAlbedo' mixes the biomes' colours.
//
// HOW THE MASKS FEED groundcover. groundcover's GroundMaps hold eight signals: height, slope (degrees),
// moisture, exposure, path distance, water distance, canopy, object distance. 'groundSignals' resamples a
// MaskSet into exactly those rasters, same units, same texel convention (texel (i, j) at the window's origin
// + (i + 0.5, j + 0.5) texels), ready to be copied into a GroundMaps with its 'set'; path, canopy and object
// come back as "none near" for the world to fill. There is one set of signals, and terraingen supplies the
// land's share of it. No package depends on the other.
//
// FILES. Only this root imports. gpu/ holds the GPU-safe half (modules 'shape' and 'erosion', compiled for
// the CPU into the package and for the GPU into terraingen.spv); src/ the rest.
//   heightfield.cone   Heightfield: a grid of heights, sample, normal, slope, tile
//   generate.cone      the CPU's generate
//   erode.cone         ErosionPlan; erodeCpu, erodeGpu
//   hydrology.cone     analyze, carveRivers, RiverSpec, RiverMaps
//   masks.cone         Biome, MaskSpec, MaskSet, computeMasks, groundSignals, bakeAlbedo
//   landscape.cone     TerrainSpec, Landscape, buildLandscape(On), generateGpu, tiles, the presets
//
// CONVENTIONS: geomath's and rigidcollide's (right-handed, y up, metres, f32); x east, z south; a
// sample (ix, iz) is at (originX + ix cell, originZ + iz cell), stored row by row.
//
// WHAT IS NOT HERE (yet): wind or glacial erosion, rivers carved by splines (roads, canals), tiled or
// infinite worlds (a Heightfield is one grid; terrainlod's tiles and a height closure serve the rest),
// a sea of its own (a sea level in the spec only lifts a flat water plane; oceanwaves is the sea), a
// GPU hydrology or GPU masks (the drainage analysis, 31 ms and run twice, and the masks, 58 ms, are CPU
// code: 120 ms of the 430 ms the 384 x 384 alpine landscape takes, the rest the GPU's erosion).

mod terraingen;

import libc;
import collections use *;
import geomath use *;
import noise;
import time;
import gpu;
import gpuwork;

pub use shape Layer, ShapeParams, Grid, heightAt, MAX_LAYERS;
pub use erosion ErosionParams, flux, waterStep, tilt, erode, advect, thermal;
